ng that's happening in  the united states right now with racial  justice you know  like there's a whole set of people that  they're never gonna change their minds  they're never going to believe that  there's  such a thing as white privilege or that  african americans  are suffering in systemic racism forget  them  they're never going to change but maybe  some people in the middle that we can  cajole and tweak and cokes and reason  with  that uh might come on board back in the  in the united states at the moment  you're experiencing wildfires you're  experiencing  really hot temperatures do you think  that those sorts of conditions  make people think twice data doesn't  work  but for some reason these extreme  cataclysmic weather events  that weren't happening with this kind of  intensity and regularity  20 or 30 years ago are starting to shift  the needle and starting to shift the  dial the rest of america just like the  uk europe and the rest of the world  has been severely affected by  coronavirus how much do you think that's  thrown people  off course in terms of really gathering  momentum to deal with climate change  obviously an international pandemic that  is  actively killing people uh is going to  take priority and it should take  priority  it's not an either or okay it's yes it's  a priority but there is  is not like let's forget climate change  let's forget racial justice and only  work on the pandemic right now no  we need to work on all of these things  and if anything  the pandemic can be this is a test run  for humanity  working together to overcome issues of  great odds  uh of great scope a great international  scope  we can do this as a human species  inhabiting this planet  not as the denizens of bolivia and the  netherlands and mongolia all just  working in their little  fiefdoms but working together  internationally after  this pandemic we can't go back to normal  we really we really cannot afford to go  back to normal  this affords us an opportunity for a  giant  reset you know a giant reboot uh  of our priorities uh moving forward in  the absence of  any kind of global leadership or  leadership from central governments  how important is it do you think for  individuals to take action  this is my favorite topic of all and  this is one of the most important topics  there is  this is the trouble with climate change  and it and it sucks  sorry there's no better word for it is  that i  as an individual can't do very much and  that is incredibly frustrating  so you have a lot of people thinking  well i'm just one person  i can eat beef you know i can have lots  of food waste i can drive a  you know an old truck that creates a lot  of diesel exhaust  what difference do i make i'm just this  is seven billion people on the planet  giant corporations and giant governments  what difference do i make  but you make a huge difference we make a  huge difference we all make a huge  difference because yes one person  doesn't  uh tip the scales but you know a  thousand  maybe ten thousand does then if a  hundred thousand people are all doing  the same thing  if a million people quit eating beef if  10 million people  start to reduce their their carbon  footprint if a hundred million people  start changing their ways  start commuting with bicycles instead of  with cars  all of a sudden we can see an impact so  we need to shift our behaviors we need  to take our friends and family  and co-workers and facebook friends and  everyone else along with us  and we are going to need to make  personal sacrifices  we love in the western world to have a  life of total convenience where we can  hit a button on amazon and anything we  want shows up at our doorstep a day or  two later  but we're going to have to shift things  to have a longer term  view we've got to plant trees support  environmental organizations  and most importantly we have to vote for  science  vote for people that are behind the  science do you feel optimistic  i feel optimistic that  big changes can be made and we can get  ourselves out of the worst  of this um it's very easy to get  pessimistic when you look at  the data and we look at some of the  things that happen  but there is a movement among young  people and that's where i feel my  greatest optimism  is what we just explored in the idiot's  guide to climate change  is that this leadership like greta  tonberg who i got to interview  and katie heider and so many other  people that  young women people in their teens in  their 20s are leading the charge  and helping make the change and we need  to get out of the way  allow them to lead and that's really  inspiring the amount of young people  that are behind the kind of drastic  change that humanity is going to need to  make  to fix this thing
 

